{"what":"The V4 positions holding liquidity in the five baseline pools, as stored by the original concentration run on 2026-09-24 (conc.py, concentration.json in the session scratch directory), with the liquidity it read for each. backend/scripts/te_concentration_baseline.py checks the live position set at the baseline block against this file.","chain_id":4663,"pool_manager":"0x8366a39cc670b4001a1121b8f6a443a643e40951","row":["owner_contracts index (the sender in the position key)","tickLower","tickUpper","salt: an integer where the sender is the V4 PositionManager, since it is then the NFT token id; otherwise the 32-byte salt as hex","liquidity"],"position_key":"keccak256(sender ++ int24 tickLower ++ int24 tickUpper ++ salt)","why_no_provider_addresses":"The sender in a V4 position key is the contract that called modifyLiquidity. The PoolManager accepts that call only inside unlock(), which calls back unlockCallback on msg.sender, so an account with no code cannot open a position. An EIP-7702 delegated account can: its code is 0xef0100 followed by the address it delegates to, so it has code and is still an externally owned account. The script therefore checks every sender's code and flags both no code and the 0xef0100 prefix; at the last run there were none of either. Where the sender is the PositionManager the salt is the NFT token id, so the provider's address is one ownerOf call away and is left to that call rather than written here.","positions":897,"owner_contracts":["0x58daec3116aae6d93017baaea7749052e8a04fa7","0x3d79c2fbb996c05ef87794606e3a44fea73d0003","0x8f78c1a2a68e7126691630ae05c36a217941af36","0xf59df59a3b7a906f2e7e0b7430103bf0710c33fb","0x27419c42e89b71dc2b260c5461990f1beeb46d4e","0x00000000000005c3287f136ef5af56c68ea6849f","0xbae15c1ce4b2ab410c4168697e12db6b1752796d","0x1a6cb635b4e9ad19653c789940f01905c602b294","0x68f6cf4baffa58e7ad9249cf33ca9dd6608d2c3e","0xc6b01f1b4fdb227321ca7cfd954f739012619a9d","0xc21f888b3583019f2356fd172ec7bb0d2dead0c7","0x0788